  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why do the wheels need to turn at different speeds round a corner?",
      "a": "On a bend, the outside wheel follows a bigger circle than the inside wheel, so it covers more ground in the same time and must turn faster.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the outer wheel go further?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the ring gear do?",
      "a": "The ring gear is a large bevel gear turned by the drive shaft's pinion. It turns the motion through a right angle and carries the cage of spider gears round with it.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the crown wheel?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do spider gears do?",
      "a": "Spider gears sit in the cage between the two side gears. Going straight they hold both wheels at the same speed; on a bend they spin so one wheel can go faster and the other slower.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a differential work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does one wheel spin in mud?",
      "a": "An open differential always gives both wheels the same turning force, limited by the wheel with least grip. If one wheel is on mud, it spins easily and the other gets almost nothing.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a limited slip differential?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where else are differentials used?",
      "a": "In almost every car, truck and tractor, in some robots and toy cars, and even in ancient Chinese south-pointing chariots.",
      "asks": [
        "Do all cars have a differential?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In an open differential, the ring gear's speed is the average of the two wheel speeds.",
    "Going straight, the spider gears do not rotate on their own pins."
  ],
